  "narrative": "The discovery batch produced three unambiguous, large associations in the claimed direction. H1 shows MSI-high cells have much stronger WRN dependency; H2 shows SMARCA4-loss cells have much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H10 shows TP53-loss cells have much weaker USP7 dependency. The signed intervals are far from zero and, converted to raw exposed-minus-comparator differences, the effects are roughly -0.79, -0.78, and +0.40 dependency-score units respectively. These are not just statistically large; relative to comparator means they are well above the 10% clinical-significance benchmark. I therefore accept H1, H2, and H10 for this dataset, while retaining them as active for robustness follow-up rather than claiming causality or cross-cohort generalizability.\n\nThe MYC-amplification comparisons H8 and H12 are null and tiny. The direct driver-gene hypotheses are mostly null or tiny: H4 KRAS-mutation/KRAS dependency is about a 6% relative difference with an interval crossing zero; H6 ERBB2 amplification/ERBB2 dependency is essentially zero; H7 BRAF V600E/BRAF dependency points slightly opposite but is small; H9 RIT1/KRAS-mutation is tiny; H3 TP53-loss/MDM2 dependency is only about 3% relative, despite a prior biological expectation. I reject these as unsupported in this dataset. H5 EGFR-amplification/EGFR dependency is the only borderline case: the point estimate is about a 10.2% relative stronger dependency, but the 95% interval includes zero. I leave H5 unresolved and request independent validation for it because a validation result would directly determine whether this borderline EGFR signal is worth adding to the accepted set.\n\nH11 is important as an anti-hypothesis finding. The original claim expected KEAP1 loss to strengthen NFE2L2 dependency, but the signed estimate is strongly negative, meaning the raw contrast is large and positive: KEAP1-loss cells show weaker NFE2L2 dependency score, roughly +0.80 relative to comparator and over 300% relative to the comparator mean. This rejects H11 as originally specified. Rather than discard the signal, I registered a reversed follow-up claim with parent H11 so the opposite association can be assessed under a directionally correct hypothesis. I also registered adenocarcinoma-restricted robustness follow-ups for the three accepted large effects and one KRAS co-loss follow-up to test whether the KRAS direct null hides context-specific effects. No validation was requested for the large accepted effects because internal evidence is already decisive; H5 is the most decision-relevant uncertain claim.",
